plan Resources
title   Resources
status  proposed
goal
    Read, acquire, refresh, and update canonical resource data through exact named families.
    Share data within one Runtime while keeping actor work and previews independently owned.
    This proposed Plan is pending approval. It establishes no shipped implementation claim.
outcomes
    O1  Exact descriptors, executable inputs, and canonical keys
        A resource owns its ID, synchronous key projector, lookup adapter, placeholder metadata,
        K-derived tags, and freshness/collection policies. The exact family and type guarantees
        appear below. Preserve P/K/A/E/R through descriptor, named O, machine, app, Runtime,
        fixture, and Story. No public support aliases are implied. Runtime requirements remain
        inferred R. persist defaults false; true opts committed canonical data into Persistence.

        resource(config) constructs an inert descriptor with these accepted authoring fields:

        | Field | Required | Accepted relationship |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | id | Yes | Exact authored descriptor ID |
        | key | Yes | (params:P) => K, synchronously |
        | lookup | Yes | (params:P, { signal }) => Effect.Effect<A,E,R> |
        | persist | No | boolean; omitted means false |
        | staleTime | No | Descriptor freshness duration; exact input domain remains @Q1 |
        | gcTime | No | Descriptor collection duration; exact input domain remains @Q1 |

        lookup receives the supported AbortSignal and preserves its exact success A, failure E,
        and service requirements R. Omitting optional fields does not authorize new freshness or
        retention defaults. @Q1 blocks the missing public type and mapper-container forms.

        The canonical key rules below define the complete key grammar, defensive copying/freezing,
        UTF-8 bytes, raw UTF-16 record ordering, diagnostics, and exact 16-level/256-node/8,192-byte
        bounds. Project and validate before ownership, admission, mutation, or work. Equal bytes
        share identity only for the exact descriptor in one Runtime. Invalid input changes nothing.
        K never reconstructs executable P or authorizes execution. Descriptor admission and shared
        identities obey @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O1 and @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O4. Durable
        opt-in follows @plan.Persistence.O1.

        ResourceFamily<P, K extends unknown[], A, E> has exactly these methods:

        | Method | Exact signature |
        | --- | --- |
        | key | (params: P): K |
        | getData | (key: K): A \| undefined |
        | getState | (key: K): ResourceState<A,E,K> |
        | lookup | (params: P, options?: FiniteResourceOptions<A,E>): FiniteOperationPlan |
        | subscribe | (params:P,options?:ResourceSubscriptionOptions<A,E>):ContinuingOperationPlan |
        | refetch | (params: P, options?: FiniteResourceOptions<A,E>): FiniteOperationPlan |
        | setData | (key:K,value:A\|((current:A\|undefined)=>A\|undefined)):CacheWritePlan |
        | cancel | (key: K): CancellationPlan |

        ResourceState<A,E,K> is the exact closed union below. Every lane has key:K.
        ResourceRetention<A> is exactly { data?: never } or { data: A }. Fields absent from a lane
        remain absent; retention applies only where stated.

        | status | Other exact fields |
        | --- | --- |
        | missing | No generation or data. |
        | pending | generation:number; no data. |
        | ready | generation:number; data:A. |
        | refreshing | generation:number; data:A. |
        | failure | generation:number; error:E; intersect ResourceRetention<A>. |
        | defect | generation:number; defect:unknown; intersect ResourceRetention<A>. |
        | interrupted | generation:number; intersect ResourceRetention<A>. |

        `undefined` is not A. It means absent `getData` or updater decline. Refreshing always
        retains data; failure, defect, and interruption may retain it. Absent fields stay absent.
        Generations are safe non-negative integers; admitted generations begin at one.

        Finite options map `success(A)`, `failure(E)`, `defect()`, and `interrupt()`. Continuing
        resource options map `value(A)`, `failure(E)`, `defect()`, and `interrupt()`. Each mapper
        returns one event from the machine's exact event protocol. Mappers receive no Exit, Cause,
        state, or lifecycle. The typed failure mapper is unavailable when E is never. Planned
        cancellation/release/supersession does not invoke an authored interruption mapper.
        These mapper names and arguments are fixed; their caller-written option container remains
        unresolved at @Q1. Illustrative examples cannot choose that container.

        The [consumer reference](references/README.md) demonstrates resource declarations and passive reads.
        Concrete application names and durations establish no defaults.

        The exact `FiniteResourceOptions`, `ResourceSubscriptionOptions`, and plan aliases above
        name semantic roles. They do not introduce additional methods, overloads, or public support
        aliases.

        Canonical operation keys:

        P is complete executable input. It may contain clients or functions and is never identity. K
        is the ordered tuple returned synchronously by `key(P)`. Descriptor ID plus canonical K
        identifies an operation within one Runtime. K does not reconstruct P, replace capabilities,
        or authorize work. Every result-changing discriminator belongs in K, including capability,
        tenant, account, network, permission, session, and any other input that can change results.
        Runtime partitioning never replaces a discriminator in K. Secrets remain observable unless
        the application hashes or replaces them before key projection.

        Project, validate, recursively copy, and freeze K into Flow-owned containers before
        acquiring ownership, admitting work, mutating state, or calling an adapter. Own the top
        tuple and every nested container. Mutation of source inputs cannot change canonical
        identity.

        Allowed values are null, booleans, finite numbers, strings, ordinary dense arrays, and plain
        records. Records have Object.prototype or null prototype and enumerable own data properties.
        Arrays have no holes or extra properties. Record keys sort by raw UTF-16 order. Numbers use
        finite JSON.stringify; negative zero becomes numeric zero. Strings receive no Unicode
        normalization.

        ```text
        KBytes ::= "[" [ Value *( "," Value ) ] "]"
        Value  ::= "null" | "true" | "false" | Number | String
        | "[" [ Value *( "," Value ) ] "]"
        | "{" [ Member *( "," Member ) ] "}"
        Member ::= String ":" Value
        ```

        KBytes is whitespace-free canonical JSON-like text encoded with the shared portable UTF-8
        boundary. This key ordering differs from the artifact codec's UTF-8 ordering. UTF-16 string
        length is not a byte length. Node Buffer is not an allowed shared encoder. Reject lone
        surrogates.

        Read own data descriptors only. Never run getters, coercion, `toJSON`, or user iteration.
        Reject undefined, non-finite numbers, bigint, symbols, functions, accessors, classes,
        unsupported objects, cycles, branded secrets, sparse/extra-property arrays, and hostile or
        inconsistent reflection. Reject before any ownership, admission, mutation, publication, or
        external work.

        The exact bounds are 16 nested levels, 256 scalar/container value nodes, and 8,192 UTF-8
        KBytes. Failures identify the exact `K[index]` or nested-record path and distinguish
        structural failures from application-domain failures. There is no custom key hash, custom
        equality, second key, or recursive type-level interpretation of runtime keys.

        Equal bytes share identity; different bytes separate it. Equal-key owners do not switch a
        running generation's pinned P. Automatic generations choose the oldest eligible live
        binding; explicit refetch uses caller P. Releasing the binding that supplied P does not
        change the running P.

        Planned identity proofs include record-order equivalence, negative-zero normalization,
        source mutation, cycles/reflection, each bound, and exact ASCII/two/three/four-byte UTF-8
        vectors. Composed/decomposed spellings stay distinct; lone-surrogate vectors reject. These
        proofs are unbuilt for the proposed capability contract.
    O2  One canonical data view with passive actor-bound readers
        One Runtime owns one canonical resource truth: committed bases, metadata, generations,
        freshness, and revisions. Same-runtime equal descriptor/K shares canonical data and lookup
        generations. Separate Runtimes, SSR requests, tests, and browser roots remain isolated.
        Actors independently own bindings, occurrences, projections, previews, and lifetimes. An
        actor's release cannot change another actor's lifetime or effective preview.

        Named key/getData/getState readers and reading a descriptor are passive. Constructing an
        unreturned operation plan is passive. Any actor may read an admitted same-runtime resource
        it never materialized. Missing reads synthesize missing state without inserting an entry,
        acquiring a lease, changing freshness, collecting, advancing revision, or starting work.
        Synchronous readers run no Effect, Scope acquisition, scheduler, or runtime mutation.

        The exact ResourceState union under @O1 owns field presence. Refreshing retains usable data;
        failure/defect/interruption may retain it. Placeholder is descriptor-owned projection
        metadata and creates no canonical write, family, or mapped outcome. Unbound reads and
        transaction updaters use committed base. Actor-bound snapshots add only that actor's ordered
        preview layers under @plan.Transactions.O3. Reads cannot create a second cache or snapshot
        authority.

        Base, generation, freshness, invalidation, hydration, and eviction changes advance canonical
        revision. Preview apply/remove/replay affects only the initiating actor's publication.
        For actor-initiated canonical writes, affected actors observe the change through complete
        current projections only after the initiating snapshot publishes, its immutable TurnRecord
        is accepted as evidence, and its private acknowledgment settles, in that order.
        Stale/unrelated revision facts publish nothing; skipped revisions
        require rereading current state. Only adjacent projection-only facts may coalesce.
        Lifecycle, cancellation, finite terminal, stream terminal, and mapped-event facts retain
        their boundaries. Publication and causal completeness obey @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7
        and @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O6. No simultaneous graph-wide publication is promised.
        Autonomous freshness/collection commits may have no initiating actor. Capture cannot observe
        a half-published actor/store commit under @plan.Persistence.O3.
    O3  Finite acquisition and continuing ownership preserve freshness and settlement
        Lookup/refetch is finite and admitted only by accepted event actions under
        @plan.Statecharts.O3. Subscribe is continuing ownership from activities or onMemory under
        @plan.Statecharts.O4. Activation, reads, timers, memory/store revisions, and completion
        cannot admit finite work implicitly. Resource cancellation is an accepted actor action.

        Fresh canonical lookup may settle without an adapter. Stale, missing, or invalidated lookup
        follows generation policy. Refetch forces replacement using caller P. A running generation
        retains its original complete P, including when its supplying binding releases. Automatic
        generations choose the oldest eligible live binding. Key-only hydrated entries remain
        passive until live P exists. Normal collection follows descriptor policy; releasing an actor
        binding removes its local projection and never itself deletes canonical data. Do not invent
        staleTime/gcTime defaults or unconditional idle retention.

        Continuing identity is compiled declaration slot, operation kind, exact descriptor, and K.
        Equal normalized declarations retain work and P. A changed slot/kind/descriptor/K releases
        the old binding once before replacement. False/null/exit releases only its declaration.
        Actor ownership and lifecycle follow @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O4 and
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5. Shared lookup work is interrupted only at its final owner.
        First/middle owner cancellation leaves remaining owners and canonical data intact.

        Activation maps an existing canonical value once. Later canonical replacements map value;
        equal effective values and preview-only changes emit no continuing value. Every finite
        occurrence still settles once, including equal-value or fresh-cache success. Outcome
        classification follows @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O2 and the exact mappers under @O1. Planned
        cancel/release/supersession records cleanup without invoking an authored interruption
        mapper. Adapter interruption, typed failure, defect, and release failure remain distinct.
        Accepted cancel(K) interrupts each current finite occurrence this actor owns for K and
        suppresses its late result. It changes no canonical data.

        Immediately before completion publication, match actor incarnation, kind, descriptor,
        canonical K, occurrence ordinal, and shared generation/lease authority. Requested
        interruption alone is no fence. Late results/finalizers may settle their bounded evidence
        but cannot mutate current data, collected entries, replacement bindings, or another actor.
        Prepared revisions start at zero; first generation/value revision is one. A new collected
        entry lifetime may restart these local cursors. Actor/global counters never reset.
        Safe-integer headroom includes terminal publication and cleanup before admission/restore;
        exhaustion fails before mutation, wrap, reuse, or partial publication.

        Offline state never blocks explicit lookup/subscribe/refetch or cancels work. Reconnect or
        focus may refresh only active subscriptions that are stale/missing and lack an in-flight
        generation. Explicit after-refresh events schedule after settlement; failure waits one
        interval. There is no automatic retry, poll helper, or generic paused-resource policy.
    O4  Authoritative writes and bounded invalidation publish atomically
        Lookup success installs canonical data. Transaction/stream output is canonical only through
        an explicit accepted setData plan. setData takes exact K and A or an updater of committed
        A|undefined. Undefined updater output declines. Validate all plans before publication. An
        accepted write fences older lookup generations, clears invalidation/failure metadata,
        advances canonical revision/generation, and publishes once with subscribers still attached.
        Equal writes may refresh freshness and publish revision; only effective-value changes map
        continuing value. Explicit completion writes publish before their mapped outcome.

        Invalidate and clear are scoped actor actions. Targets are exact [O.resource,K], declared
        reachable tags, or admitted resource families. Validate every target, expand against one
        pre-mutation index in descriptor/K insertion order, and first-seen deduplicate. Reject the
        whole action for invalid/unauthorized/conflicting targets or expansion above 256 identities,
        before mutation or work allocation. Missing exact targets and zero matches succeed as
        no-ops.

        A nonempty invalidate retains data and previews, marks stale, and changes no running
        generation. It starts no lookup directly. A nonempty clear fences work, removes data,
        metadata, and previews, and interrupts according to final-owner policy. Surviving
        subscriptions observe missing and may reacquire normally. Each nonempty action publishes one
        atomic canonical revision. There is no partial valid prefix or changing target set.

        Boot/SSR/fixture writes use the construction-owned boundary in @plan.ReactAndHosts.O4; they
        introduce no public writer or finite occurrence. Runtime disposal, rather than an ordinary
        cache-clear escape hatch, owns complete lifetime removal.
decisions
    D1  Canonical data and actor work have separate ownership
        Equal descriptor/K shares committed truth within one Runtime. Actor-local previews and work
        retain independent lifetimes, so cancellation cannot erase another actor's truth. Internal
        store, fanout, barrier, and Effect primitive choices remain replaceable.
    D2  Exact named families are the operation boundary
        Execution consumes P; readers consume K. Only returned accepted action/continuing plans
        execute. The inline family/state forms fix consumer shape without exposing registries.
constraints
    C1  Keep passive reads and ownership separate
        Add no generic registry, resources.get, transactions.get, operation enumeration, bound ref,
        byKey/byLane/require, public occurrence handle, second cache, or selector execution method.
        Add no read-triggered subscription, retry/reset/seed/retention/mutation helper.
    C2  Preserve explicit targeting and accepted policy
        Add no wildcard, whole-runtime, zero-argument, resource-family invalidate method, partial
        mixed batch, custom hash/equality, cross-runtime sharing, or invented freshness default. No
        browser-offline admission gate or implicit canonical result promotion is supported.
verification
    V1  Exact API and key proofs
        Establish @O1 and the state/type portion of @O2. Planned, unbuilt checks belong at
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/operation/test/key.test.ts` and
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/operation/test/resource-types.test.ts`. Compile positive
        P/K/A/E/R inference through all consuming boundaries and negative calls for widened/missing
        P, wrong K, E=never failure mapper, generic readers, and extra methods. Narrow every
        ResourceState lane and reject absent-field access. Compile required constructor fields,
        each accepted optional field, and exact adapter P/A/E/R. Reject renamed fields and wrong
        callback relationships. Exact duration/metadata typing and wrong mapper-container cases
        remain blocked by @Q1; no passing type proof is claimed for those forms.

        Run key grammar, reflection, source-mutation, byte-order, surrogate, and each exact bound
        vector under @O1. Pass when invalid keys leave store, ownership, ordinals, publication, and
        adapter calls unchanged. Equal keys must share identity despite source record insertion
        order; permission and every other result-changing discriminator must separate unequal
        result identity. Distinct Unicode bytes must remain distinct. These are planned
        executable proofs, not compilation receipts for the examples.
    V2  Canonical sharing and passive projections
        Establish @O2. Planned, unbuilt check:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/operation/test/resource-store.test.ts`. Share exact keys
        across two actors and isolate two Runtimes/SSR requests/browser roots. Read missing and
        never-owned entries repeatedly; pass with no allocation, lease, work, revision, freshness,
        or collection change. Compare base/owner-preview/other-actor reads. Release one binding
        without deleting canonical truth or the other binding.

        Interleave writes, delayed revision notifications, and capture. Pass when no affected actor
        observes an actor-initiated write before the initiating snapshot publishes, its immutable
        TurnRecord is accepted as evidence, and its private acknowledgment settles, in that order.
        Recipients reread current truth; stale/unrelated notifications publish nothing, skipped
        revisions never reconstruct a false history, and capture sees no half cut.
        Check canonical revisions separately from owner-only preview publications.
    V3  Acquisition, cancellation, and freshness races
        Establish @O3. Planned, unbuilt check:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/operation/test/resource-lifecycle.test.ts`. Cover
        fresh/stale/missing/invalidated lookup, forced refetch, pinned P and oldest binding, equal
        declaration retention, changed identity, false/null/exit, and first/middle/final owner. Pass
        when finite equal-value success settles once while continuing equal values stay silent.
        Adapter starts must match accepted actions/declarations exactly. Passive reads and merely
        constructed plans must start none. Test offline admission and eligible reconnect/focus.

        Race cancel/replacement/disposal/collection with success, failure, defect, and finalizers.
        Pass with no stale data/event/publication and exactly one release/settlement. Planned
        cleanup must never call an interruption mapper. Force safe-integer exhaustion before
        admission and restore; pass with terminal/cleanup credits preserved and no partial state.
    V4  Writes and invalidation batch proofs
        Establish @O4. Planned, unbuilt check:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/operation/test/resource-writes.test.ts`. Test explicit
        writes against old generation completions, updater decline, equal writes, retained
        subscribers, placeholder/tag purity, and completion-write-before-outcome ordering. Pass with
        one accepted revision, current truth protected, and no implicit result promotion.

        Exercise exact/tag/family/mixed targets, stable order/deduplication, conflicting changes,
        invalid targets, zero matches, exactly 256 targets, and one over the bound. Pass when each
        invalid batch changes nothing; each nonempty valid batch publishes once. Invalidate retains
        data/previews and starts no lookup. Clear removes/fences atomically; surviving subscriptions
        see missing. Verify construction writes separately through @plan.ReactAndHosts.O4.
questions
    Q1  Which missing resource authoring types and mapper containers are accepted?
        Required id/key/lookup and optional persist/staleTime/gcTime are fixed above. K-derived
        tags and passive placeholder projections remain accepted metadata capabilities.
        Their exact authoring forms remain unresolved. The sources do not settle:

        - The caller-written finite and continuing mapper containers: direct members or outcomes.
        - Exact tag/placeholder authoring fields, containers, and callback input/result types.
        - Complete staleTime/gcTime input types, Infinity acceptance, and omitted policy behavior.

        Keep these forms unresolved. Add no defaults or legacy callback/route options by inference.
